{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>If it was done unwittingly from the eyes of the congregation.</b> If this transgression was committed from the eyes of the congregation through inadvertence — that they erred and ruled permitting one of the forms of idolatry-service that is [in fact] forbidden to be served as idolatry by it (there, v. 5)." "<b>לחטת.</b> Missing an alef — because it is not like other sin-offerings: for all sin-offerings in the Torah that come together with a burnt-offering, the sin-offering precedes the burnt-offering, as it says (Leviticus 5), ״וְאֶת הַשֵּׁנִי יַעֲשֶׂה עֹלָה״ — but this one, the burnt-offering precedes the sin-offering (there, 15)."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {0 "Rashi's comment assumes the Beit Aharon edition's punctuation; the parenthetical citation '(שם ה׳)' refers back to an earlier passage. The phrase 'מותרת לעבוד ע״ז בכך' is rendered hyper-literally: 'permitted to be served as idolatry by it' — i.e., the court mistakenly ruled that this particular mode of service was permitted for idolatry, when in fact it is forbidden.", 1 "The lemma לחטת is written deficient (without alef) in certain manuscripts of Numbers 15:24, where the full form לחטאת usually appears. Rashi's cross-reference '(שם ט״ו)' points to Numbers 15:15 in the chapter under discussion, not Leviticus 15."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:05.833856066Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}